  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively connect temperature-dependent mechanical or tribological testing of HEAs with dislocation structures, stacking faults, FCC–HCP transformations, recrystallization, and lattice ordering or disordering. In particular, R1/R11 identify a temperature-dependent change in deformation mechanisms and dislocation-based strengthening in a closely related FCC HEA; R15 predicts suppression of stacking faults as temperature rises; R18 supplies temperature-dependent molecular-dynamics deformation of a closely related HEA with FCC–HCP and twinning mechanisms; and R5 demonstrates temperature-dependent wear of an HEA film. These clues point toward studying thermally driven changes in nanoscale mechanical and friction behavior. However, none discloses the specific FeNiCrCoCu-on-Cu coating, combined nanoindentation/friction simulation, or the proposed high-temperature sequence of dislocation-density loss, rapid lattice disorder, softening above about 900 K, and rising friction. Selecting that particular intervention and mechanism would therefore require a substantial new idea."
}
